Go's founders looked at exception systems and rejected them for Go. Instead, an error is an ordinary value — usually the last return — and every caller decides, right at the call, what to do about it. The function signature itself tells you it can fail; there's no invisible throw path to go hunting for.

```go
f, err := os.Open(path)
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("load config: %w", err)
}
```

That explicitness is the design. With exceptions, a call that can fail looks identical to one that can't — the failure exits through a side channel the compiler doesn't force you to acknowledge. With (value, error), the check is three lines you can see, and unused-variable rules mean you can't accidentally ignore the error entirely without the compiler noticing.

The cost is honest: Go code has more visible if err != nil branches than equivalent exception code has catch blocks. But the branches are where the handling actually lives, and their visibility is what keeps failure paths reviewed. You get to complain about the verbosity; you don't get surprise control flow.

Why do these conventions hold so strictly? Because they compose. Consider a caller three layers up:

```go
func loadUser(id string) (*User, error) {
    row, err := db.Query(id)     // convention: err nil → row valid
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err           // convention: on failure, nil result
    }
    return row.toUser(), nil      // convention: success → nil error
}
```

Every function obeying the conventions makes the next one simpler: the caller's job is always check-then-use, wrap-then-return. One function breaking them — returning a usable value alongside a non-nil error — forces every caller to read its docs to know whether the value is usable. Conventions fail when they're convenient to break; these survive because breaking them taxes everyone downstream.

The zero-value-on-error rule has one deliberate exception in the standard library: io.Reader's Read returns the count of bytes actually read BEFORE the error (including io.EOF). That's documented, universal, and the one case where partial results plus error is the right contract — data is precious, so you keep what you got even as you report the failure. Note how the exception proves the design: it exists only where data loss would otherwise occur, and it's documented everywhere it applies.

A Go function returns one error value — but that one value can represent several failures. Before Go 1.20 you handled this with fmt.Errorf and %w, which wraps exactly one cause; truly parallel failures (this batch item 3 failed AND item 7 failed) needed a custom multi-error type.

Go 1.20 standardized it: errors.Join takes any number of errors and returns a single error that reports all of them, unwraps to each, and matches each with errors.Is. The joined error's message is the components' messages joined with newlines.

```go
var errs []error
for _, t := range tasks {
    if err := t.Run(); err != nil {
        errs = append(errs, fmt.Errorf("task %s: %w", t.Name, err))
    }
}
if len(errs) > 0 {
    return errors.Join(errs...)
}
```

The caller still sees ONE error from the function — the convention holds — but errors.Is can find any component cause, and the printed message includes every failure. The batch pattern (run everything, report everything that failed, not just the first) is the main use: fail-fast wastes the information about what else would have failed.

Before 1.20 and in code that must support older versions, the same shape is built by hand: a small struct with an Error() method that concatenates, and an Unwrap() []error method — which is exactly what Join provides for you now.

The design pressure shows best at the boundaries. Consider a config loader:

```go
// Option A: panics on bad config — callers get crashes for input problems
// Option B: returns (Config, error) — callers decide: exit, default, retry
func Load(path string) (Config, error)
```

Option B is forced honesty: the API author had to decide which failures belong to the caller. That decision is API design. Option A offloads it to whoever hits the panic — the caller becomes the error handler by surprise, with less context than the library had.

The same forcing appears in constructors. A constructor that can fail must return (T, error) — so the type's zero value becomes a design question: is a zero Config usable (use a default) or invalid (require New)? Go's answer varies by type — usable zero values are idiomatic (bytes.Buffer works zero-constructed) where possible, and the (T, error) constructor exists for the cases where validation is mandatory. Neither escape hatch exists in exception-based APIs; the signature can't pose the question. That's the pattern's real gift to API design: the failure contract is a first-class, typed part of the interface, not folklore in the docs.
